# The drive over CAN (docs/can_support.md) instead of smart-serial: the
# drive's can component (shared/comps/can.c, template "can") on a simulated
# bus at 1 Mbit/s, LinuxCNC's end as it runs on the Raspberry Pi
# (linuxcnc/stmblcan: its bus code and MCP2515 driver on a simulated
# MCP2515), a 1 kHz servo period. The burn-in motor (tests/can/stmbl.conf).
# - enable, phasing, a constant-velocity move: feedback one period behind
# - every period's feedback received; ~325 us of bus time per period
# - the console over the CAN frames: about
# - the drive taken off the bus: it times out (CMD error, soft fault),
#   LinuxCNC sees it not present and its frames unacknowledged (tx_late);
#   back on: connected, and enabled again after LinuxCNC toggles enable
@args --drive tests/can/stmbl.conf
@timeout 120
lcnccan 1
lcnc 0 0
run 0.5
can0.connected
lcnc 1 0
run 1.5
fault0.state
lcnc 1 31.4
run 1
@sample pid0.pos_error 0.001 0.3
hv0.sim_vel
can0.syncs
canstat
con about
run 0.5
conread
canplug 0
run 0.05
can0.connected
fault0.state
canstat
canplug 1
run 0.05
can0.connected
lcnc 0 0
run 0.2
lcnc 1 0
run 1.5
fault0.state
@expect can0.connected[1] == 1
@expect fault0.state[1] == 1
@expect hv0.sim_vel ~ 19.73 1%
@expect max|pid0.pos_error| < 0.01
@expect can0.connected[2] == 0
@expect fault0.state[2] == 3
@expect can0.connected[3] == 1
@expect fault0.state[3] == 1
@match ^can: present 1 fb (29[0-9][0-9]|3[0-9]{3}) missed 0 tx_late 0 rx_overflow 0 foreign 0
@match ^con> STMBL simulated drive
@match ^can: present 0 fb [0-9]+ missed [1-9][0-9]* tx_late [1-9]
@match Fault 1: CMD error
